      <memberdef kind="function" id="group__fms__mod_1ga05e7686befc1e49d2be9bdb81d479a76" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>integer function, public</type>
        <definition>integer function, public fms_mod::check_nml_error</definition>
        <argsstring>(IOSTAT, NML_NAME)</argsstring>
        <name>check_nml_error</name>
        <param>
          <type>IOSTAT</type>
          <defname>IOSTAT</defname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>NML_NAME</type>
          <defname>NML_NAME</defname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Checks the iostat argument that is returned after reading a namelist and determines if the error code is valid. </para>        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><simplesect kind="return"><para>This function returns the input iostat value (integer) if it is an allowable error code. If the iostat error code is not allowable, an error message is printed and the program terminated.</para></simplesect>
The FMS allows multiple namelist records to reside in the same file. Use this interface to check the iostat argument that is returned after reading a record from the namelist file. If an invalid iostat value is detected this routine will produce a fatal error. See the NOTE below.</para><para>Some compilers will return non-zero iostat values when reading through files with multiple namelist. This routine will try skip these errors and only terminate for true namelist errors.</para><para><linebreak/>
Examples<linebreak/>
</para><para>The following example checks if a file exists, reads a namelist input from that file, and checks for errors in that namelist. When the correct namelist is read and it has no errors the routine check_nml_error will return zero and the while loop will exit. This code segment should be used to read namelist files. <programlisting filename=".F90"><codeline><highlight class="keywordtype">integer</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>::<sp/>ierr,<sp/>io</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"></highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"></highlight><highlight class="keyword">read</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>(input_nml_file,<sp/>fms_nml,<sp/>iostat=io)</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">ierr<sp/>=<sp/>check_nml_error(io,</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&apos;fms_nml&apos;</highlight><highlight class="normal">)</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting> <parameterlist kind="exception"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>FATAL</parametername>
<parametername>Unknown</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>error while reading namelist ...., (IOSTAT = ####) There was an error reading the namelist specified. Carefully examine all namelist and variables for anything incorrect (e.g. malformed, hidden characters).</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>FATAL</parametername>
<parametername>Unknown</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>namelist, or mistyped namelist variable in namelist ...., (IOSTAT = ####) The name list given doesn&apos;t exist in the namelist file, or a variable in the namelist is mistyped or isn&apos;t a namelist variable.</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">iostat</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The iostat value returned when reading a namelist record.</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">nml_name</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The name of the namelist. This name will be printed if an error is encountered, otherwise the name is not used. </para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
